okay in this lesson we're going to talk  about writing equations in  slope-intercept form so just to review  here we've got y equals mx plus b this  is our slope intercept form and when  you're doing word problems or story  problems on the act you want to think  about the slope as the rate okay like  the rate of change or how much something  costs per hour you're thinking about a  rate and then the y intercept or the b  value think of that as the initial  condition or the starting amount so  let's go through a couple examples and i  get a feel for how this works so go  ahead and pause the video see if you can  do  each of these examples and we'll go  through them together so the first one  says judy has run 30 miles so far this  year  if she runs two miles a day  what equation models the number of miles  that she runs m  after w weeks okay so number of miles is  m and the number of weeks is w  okay so how would you do that one well  instead of thinking of y equals mx plus  b like we normally do we're going to  think about  miles as the output and now weeks is the  input but what we're interested in is  finding out what's the rate well she's  running two miles a day that's her rate  but in terms of weeks okay she's  actually running 14 miles  per week so that's going to be 14 times  the number of weeks but she's already  ran that's her initial condition  30 miles so far so plus 30. so for  example if 10 weeks pass  she'll run 10 times 14 140 plus the 30  that she's already run 170 miles so  again when you do these problems think  of the slope as the rate okay and the y  intercept as the starting amount or the  initial condition okay example number  two try this one on your own if after  two hours the water level is at five  feet and after five hours the water  levels at 11 feet  how many feet will the water level be at  after eight hours okay so we're assuming  that this is increasing at a  linear rate okay a constant rate  and they would tell you that in the  problem  so in this example what we can do is we  can coordinate these points what i mean  by that is you say after two hours the  water level is at five feet and after  five hours the water level's at 11 feet  and we're wondering after eight hours  how many feet is it going to be at well  when you have two points like this you  can calculate the slope okay which is  the rate of change and you would do that  by  y2 minus y1 so 11 minus 5 over x2 minus  x1 5 minus 2 so that gives us 6 over 3  which equals 2. so what that means is  rising at  2 feet  per hour  okay now if we go to put this into our  equation y equals mx plus b  we know that y equals oops do y equals  2x  plus b we don't know what the b value is  the starting  level of the water but what we can do is  we can take one of these points either 2  5 or 5 11. i'm just going to use 2 5 i'm  going to put 5 in for y  i'm going to put 2 in for x and i'm  going to solve for b so we have 5 equals  4 plus b  subtract 4 from both sides and you can  see that b equals 1. so if we put that  back in here we've got our equation so  it's  y equals two x plus one after eight  hours let's put that in for x we get two  times eight is sixteen plus one is  seventeen so the water will be at a  level of seventeen feet so that's how  you would approach these uh linear  equation problems you want to make use  of the slope intercept form of the line  and pay attention to the rate and the  initial condition sometimes you have to  solve for that initial condition by  using one of the points that we did here  in example two  so definitely something important you  want to know for the ect
 

